Now long simulation. Use Playwright eval to drive keys? Better use a JS eval that dispatches keyboard events? The game listens to window keydown with e.code. I can dispatch synthetic KeyboardEvent('keydown',{code:'KeyA'}) repeatedly; but Playwright keyboard is easier. I'll run a bash loop with sleeps and key changes via playwright-cli, which is slow due to CLI startup each call (~1s). Alternative: use eval to dispatch keys at intervals via a self-contained async script? page.evaluate supports async functions and can await new Promise(setTimeout). Yes! One eval call can run a bot for 30s: dispatch keydown, sleep, change direction, etc., and call __nova stats at the end. But mouse fire is via touchFire debug auto already. Use:

```
await page.evaluate(async () => {
  const keys = ['KeyA','KeyD','KeyW','KeyS'];
  const r = [];
  for (let i=0;i<15;i++){ const k=keys[Math.floor(Math.random()*4)]; window.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown',{code:k})); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,900)); window.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keyup',{code:k})); }
  return window.__nova.stats();
});
```

But a single key held for 900ms moves 220px. With autofire touching shards, might collect. Note the game may hit upgrade state and freeze gameplay; then stats show 'upgrade' and we can click a card. To handle, random movement kills enemies and shards may be collected. If level-up occurs, state upgrade and bot movement ignored; test ends. Good, we'll see.

Use diagonals less. Also dash occasionally via Space? The keydown Space triggers dash only if state playing; fine.

Let me run 20s with eval. Note page.evaluate awaits; Playwright default timeout 30s? The CLI might have a timeout. Keep 16s.

Let me also add debug key tracking? No.

Run.

