content.text:
Upgrade panel looks good at 1280. HUD visible behind blur (score 1104, hull 100). Nice.

But I notice the panel's backdrop blur turns background almost black, fine.

Now one card (NOVA HEART) is useless at full HP 100 (restores 25%). It says restore 25% of hull. Picking it at full HP is a trap, but offers no permanent benefit. Upgrades in such games always benefit; maybe make Nova Heart also +5 max hull. Or filter it out when at full HP. Better: change NOVA HEART to "Restore 25% hull and gain +8 max hull" — always useful. Similarly REINFORCED HULL already +10 max. Nova Heart could be "+15 max hull, restore 30". That's similar to reinforced. Alternative: "SHIELD BURST: gain a 1-hit shield"? Complex.

Simplify: replace NOVA HEART with "TIME DILATION: enemies 8% slower"? Implement a global enemySpeed multiplier on player. Could add. Or "SALVAGE: +15% score"? Hmm.

I'll make NOVA HEART: "Restore 30% hull and raise max hull by 5." Always a small benefit. Description consistent.

Also the upgrade cards' key labels 01/02/03 in the top-right and numbers work on desktop. Great.

After clicking an upgrade, the player continues. Need to verify the transition and then natural death? Not necessary.

Now, the level-up panel doesn't show current pick restrictions; fine.

Next important thing: test end-of-game natural death isn't possible easily; debug tested.

Let's improve the game over "SIGNAL LOST" check after debug hurt with fixed loop. It worked earlier.

What about restarting from the pause screen ("ABANDON RUN & RESTART")? startGame resets. Test quickly.

Also test refresh localStorage best > 0: best got 432; after the latest run score 1104 but not ended, best still 432. On end, best updates. Fine.

Now, gameplay at sector 2/3 and brutes/spitters: at 22s sector 2, at 44s sector 3. The bot run 16s only. Long run to test brute behavior + bullet collisions. Brute hp 6+1. Should work.

Potential performance: bullets, particles at 60 updates/s. Fine.

A subtle update bug: `for (let i = enemies.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) { ... if (d < ... killEnemy(i)) }` — inside the enemy loop we call killEnemy(i), which splices enemies. That's safe because iterating backwards, but then the loop continues with i-- referencing the new array; fine. However, `killEnemy` calls burst and shards, fine.

But in the shots loop, we iterate enemies backwards and call killEnemy(j), same safety.

One bug: in the shots loop, after killEnemy(j), if pierce>0 we decrement and continue; the enemy array shifted; j loop continues to j--, fine.

Sound: game start toast doesn't play sound (sound off by default). Fine.

Now, the touch HUD top bound uses W <= 760, but a landscape phone (844x390) has W 844 > 760 → desktop bounds, and touch controls are hidden because media query uses max-width 760. On a landscape phone, W=844, H=390: `@media(max-width:760px)` won't apply, so touch controls hidden and welcome layout desktop (padding 13.6%) but H 390 < min-height 500 causes the game shell to be 500px tall and overflow clipped (body overflow hidden) → broken layout on landscape phones. Hmm. Should add a landscape-mobile media query: `(max-height:520px) and (pointer:coarse)`? Playwright desktop pointer may not match. For real phones, coarse. Use `@media(max-height:560px)` to adjust the welcome layout and show touch controls? But desktop small windows 1280x500 would also show touch controls. That's acceptable? Not ideal.

Simpler: use `@media (max-width:900px), (max-height:520px)` for the mobile layout. This makes short desktop windows mobile-ish. Fine. But the game-shell min-height 500 at H 390 forces overflow; change min-height to 0 or 320. And the welcome content may overflow at 390 height (title 54px + intro + button ≈ 300). With topbar and footer, tight. Use `@media(max-height:560px)` to compact: h1 font 44px, intro margin smaller, hide annotations, hide footer, show touch controls? For a landscape phone, the touch controls should show. Hmm.

How important is landscape mobile? A complete game should handle it. Let me implement:

```
@media (max-height:600px) {
  .game-shell{min-height:0}
  .welcome-copy{margin-top:0;width:340px}
  h1{font-size:clamp(38px,9vh,58px)}
  .intro{margin:12px 0 16px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5}
  .kicker{margin-bottom:10px}
  .primary-button{height:42px}
  .best-score{margin-top:12px}
  .welcome-footer{display:none}
  .ship-annotation{display:none}
  .topbar{top:12px}
  .hud{top:60px}
  .xp-wrap{bottom:...}
}
```

But touch controls display only under max-width 760. If landscape phone width 844, `pointer:coarse` would help. Use `@media (pointer:coarse)` as an additional selector for `.touch-controls{display:block}` and related? CSS duplication complex.

Maybe keep scope: desktop + portrait mobile. The emulated test is portrait. Reasonable assumption. I could still fix min-height and allow landscape to play with keyboard? On a phone, no keyboard. Hmm.

Given time, improve landscape: use `@media (max-width:760px)` OR `(max-height:500px)`. Extract the touch controls block to apply to both: write a separate media query duplicating only `.touch-controls{display:block...}` and `.game-shell.playing .touch-controls`. The rest of the mobile layout still won't apply at width 844, but touch controls show, and the HUD is desktop style. Bounds in JS use W<=760 → player can go behind controls. Change JS mobile detection to `W <= 760 || H <= 560`. Good.

But the welcome screen at 844x390: `min-height:500` means the shell is 500 tall while viewport 390 → footer cut, button might still fit? Content centered in 500 with topbar at 28; welcome copy 400px tall might overflow. Add `@media(max-height:560px)` compact style. Let me do a reasonable compact set.

Actually, let me not over-engineer. I'll add:

```
@media(max-height:560px){
 .game-shell{min-height:0}
 .welcome-copy{width:330px;transform:scale(.82);transform-origin:left center}
 .welcome-footer{display:none}
 .ship-annotation{display:none}
 .hud{top:64px}
 .topbar{top:14px}
}
```

Scaling the welcome copy to .82 is a hack but effective. The touch controls hidden at width 844 remain. Add `@media(max-height:560px){.touch-controls{display:block;...}}` but need full styles; they're already defined in the max-width 760 block which won't apply. Duplication is ugly but small: I could change the max-width block's media query to `@media(max-width:760px),(max-height:560px)` for ALL mobile styles. That would shrink the welcome title etc. on short desktop windows too—acceptable. But the 390px override remains only max-width 390.

Changing one media query to include max-height is simple. Then a 1280x500 desktop window gets mobile UI/touch controls (since .touch-controls display block) — with mouse still working. That's a weird but functional fallback. Hmm, touch controls displayed on desktop short windows is undesirable.

Better rely on pointer coarse: `@media(max-width:760px), (pointer:coarse)`. In Playwright desktop, pointer is fine. On landscape phone, coarse matches. In CSS, `(pointer:coarse)` matches any phone, so all mobile styles apply on landscape too. That's clean! Use `@media(max-width:760px), (pointer:coarse)`. But then desktop touch laptops (coarse primary pointer) might get mobile layout; rare and still playable.

And JS mobile bounds: `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches || W <= 760`. Do that.

The min-height 500 still breaks landscape 390 height. Add `@media(max-height:560px){.game-shell{min-height:0}.welcome-copy{transform:scale(.8);...}.welcome-footer,.ship-annotation{display:none}h1{...}}`.

Will the fixed mobile CSS from the coarse query still apply correctly at 844x390? The mobile styles include welcome padding 8%, h1 clamp(55px,13vw,78px) → 844*0.13=110 clamped 78px — too tall for 390. The compact max-height query can override h1 to 44px, intro margins, etc.

Let me add a compact landscape block after the mobile query:

```
@media(max-height:560px){
 .game-shell{min-height:0}
 .topbar{top:12px}
 .hud{top:58px}
 .welcome-copy{margin-top:0;width:330px}
 h1{font-size:clamp(38px,11vh,54px)}
 .kicker{margin-bottom:10px}
 .intro{margin:12px 0 16px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5}
 .primary-button{height:44px}
 .best-score{margin-top:12px}
 .welcome-footer,.ship-annotation{display:none}
 .controls-hint{display:none}
 .xp-wrap{bottom:18px;right:auto;width:45%}
 .touch-controls{display:block} /* already via coarse */
 .modal-card{padding:22px 30px} h2{font-size:38px} .modal-copy{display:none}
 .upgrade-panel{padding:20px 30px} .upgrade-panel h2{font-size:32px} .upgrade-grid{margin-top:14px} .upgrade-card{min-height:110px}
}
```

Hmm modals at 390 height: compact card with h2 38 (~70px for 2 lines), kicker, button 44, best — around 240; fits. Upgrade panel: kicker 10+20, h2 2 lines 58, copy hidden, grid 110 + margins → ~250. fits.

The cooldown/dash etc.

But the XP wrap at bottom 18 conflicts with touch controls at bottom 21-131. In landscape, place XP at top? Keep bottom 147? H 390, bottom 18 means under controls. Set `.xp-wrap{display:none}` on short screens (progress is visible via LV? no). Or bottom:138. Left 5% etc. With touch controls only 120 tall, xp at bottom 138 is above. Set `bottom:140px`. HUD at 58 for 390 height. Play field top bound 118 (too low); use H<=560 → topBound 96, bottom H-135. Fragile but fine.

Given the user likely tests portrait/desktop, I'll implement coarse+short adjustments lightly.

Also update JS mobile detection.

Let me first fix NOVA HEART description and then these CSS/JS adjustments. Then remove debug, final test, maybe write README note? Not necessary but nice to document controls. README is OMGithub boilerplate; I could append a "Nova Drift" section. That's an expected deliverable? A game repo README describing the game is nice. Add brief section above the omgithub block? The readme has omgithub markers. Append a section at the end. Fine.

Also should there be a package.json / server? start.sh uses runtime default-start; likely serves static. The previous game worked? There was no source. The runtime's default-start may serve the directory. I ran python. Let me check what default-start.mjs does to ensure it serves index.html. If it expects a Vite app, a static index.html should still work. Let me inspect RUNTIME_DIR.

